draw a shrunk sprite and a one-colour blend a row at a time

The NES shelf still missed the latch on 80 of 600 frames after 0037, and
a menu under the dim scrim on 432 of 600. Traced on the handheld,
2026-10-31, with Config/trace-latency.txt:

    tex 2: 301x340 from 304x343 (0.99x DOWN) 3.42 ms 33 ns/px
    tex 1: 237x268 from 304x343 (0.78x DOWN) 1.67 ms 26 ns/px
    sprite stage by kind: rect 12.6 ms over 1, tex 5.6 ms over 11

Every cart but the centred one is drawn shrunk, and a shrunk draw took
blit's direct path: gather, test and copy one texel at a time, 26-33 ns a
pixel against 7-9 for a row that is gathered and memcpy'd. An untinted
shrunk draw now gathers each row into the line in one tight pass, noting
its lowest alpha, and copies the line whole when that is opaque enough --
the solid middle of a cart, which is most of its rows. Other rows take the
same per-texel rule as before.

The menus' dim scrim is one rect over the whole canvas, blended per pixel:
12.6 ms. A rect is one colour, so each channel of the blend depends on the
destination byte alone; the rect builds four 256-entry tables from blend
itself and looks each byte up.

Both are exact. blit's reference tests already cover shrinking, with rows
that are opaque, clear and mixed; a new test draws a cart-shaped face --
opaque middle, clear corners, soft edge -- at the shelf's 0.78 and 0.99 at
fractional positions and compares it with the reference byte for byte.
fill_rect's reference test covers the tables across alphas and colours.
Not yet run on the handheld.

Applies on top of next-eject-ground-on-a-thread, upstream 91499a9.

